Do not remove assertions, invent flow packages, regenerate signed artifacts, or
copy stale documents to mask these failures. The full remote-capable browser
suite, tenant integration, delivery, performance and commissioning were not run.
Local boot is not proof that all declared applications or routes work.
Actions inspection found only the current agent workflow and no failed jobs;
there was no prior failing CI run to diagnose.

Tracked-content security assessment
-----------------------------------
The root audit scans every git-tracked path across snapshots. At initial review
it examined 3,976 tracked files: 33 files matched credential-shaped patterns,
one private-config filename required review, and 21 containers were uninspected.
Counts are file counts, NOT unique, valid, or currently live credentials.
Added review files change the total tracked-file count on subsequent runs.
The old test:secrets excludes reference material and is not a public-release gate.

The new audit checks UTF-8/UTF-16 text for signed query fragments, common GitHub/
AWS key shapes and private-key markers. It does not print values or filenames.
Private-config filenames (including renamed copies), archives/office containers/
PDFs, unreadable/oversized/nonregular files and restricted agent paths are blockers.
It does not expand archives, inspect private configs semantically, cover every
secret format, or scan untracked/build output/history. A zero pattern count would
NOT establish absence of secrets. Expanded-container and comprehensive secret
review must be performed securely by an authorised owner.

Read-only specialist evidence (paths only; no bearer values reproduced):
* config.local (1).js is tracked. Its filename alone does not prove a valid secret.
* Repo_root/README.md:22-42 contains exposure/rotation/private-repository claims.
  Referenced reference-corpus and rotation/readiness documents are absent here;
  old numerical or "closed" assertions are not independent revocation evidence.
* Repo_root/config/auth.config.js:41-43 defaults authentication to inert.
  Repo_root/core/auth.js:121-148 uses local-state identity in that posture.
  Browser role controls or setting auth.enabled alone are not server security.
* Repo_root/scripts/package.mjs:224-237,443-449 accepts optional auth settings and
  records disclosed-signature reuse instead of establishing secure distribution.
  Repo_root/scripts/package-bundle.mjs:21-26 describes embedded configuration.
  Packaging routes differ; none was invoked or approved by this review.
* A separate bounded Python stdlib zipfile inspection read all 105 file members
  of the 21 tracked ZIPs in memory (885,848 uncompressed bytes). It found zero
  credential-pattern matches and no encrypted/nested/unreadable/oversized members.
  No files were extracted, names or values logged, or payloads executed. The
  inspection used a 32 MiB member/total limit and signed-query/private-key/common
  token/JWT heuristics. It does not validate payload provenance or semantics,
  credential revocation, or general secret absence. The automated root gate
  deliberately still flags containers: it does not implement that inspection.
* This is a shallow clone. Full history, other refs, forks, caches and prior
  downloads were not audited. Deletion or history rewriting revokes nothing.

Release checklist: all conditions are mandatory
-----------------------------------------------
[ ] Owner immediately reviews public visibility and restricts repository/artifact
    access as appropriate. Review permissions, public downloads, forks and caches;
    do not assume a visibility change retracts previously exposed material.
[ ] Authorised tenant administrator inventories exposed credentials across all
    snapshots, renamed configs, expanded containers, full history and other refs;
    revokes/rotates affected credentials and verifies old credentials are rejected.
    Keep values and evidence in approved private storage, never in PR logs.
[ ] Owner verifies removal/sanitisation of sensitive current-tree content and any
    necessary coordinated history remediation. Rotation and history cleanup are
    separate obligations; neither is established by this change.
[ ] Restore missing source, docs and fixtures from an authorised, provenance-checked
    source. Resolve undefined scripts and every offline failure without weakening
    tests. Re-run the root gates and securely review dependencies and containers.
[ ] Register/approve tenant identity integration and enforce authentication plus
    server-side role/record authorisation, expiry, audience/issuer and replay checks.
    Prove missing/forged/expired/wrong-role identities fail. Do not trust browser
    localStorage, userEmail, hidden controls, or the pilot's passing inert tests.
[ ] Owner authorises an isolated non-production commissioning plan with access
    restrictions, test identities/data, audit/retention/backup, rollback and signoff.
    Validate portal, workspace, vault, administration, mailer and flow contracts
    only within that separately authorised plan. No live action is authorised here.
[ ] Before any later distribution, separately review packaging paths and expanded
    outputs. Exclude private config and all signed endpoints. Do not create or
    upload a credential-bearing package, even if inherited tooling permits it.
[ ] Record dated owner/security/tenant acceptance in approved private evidence,
    explicitly reconciling historical closure assertions with this repository.

Explicit NO-GO conditions: public exposure not contained; revocation/history or
container coverage unknown; private config/signed endpoints in distributable
content; failing/missing checks or applications; inert/unenforced tenant auth;
unverified authorisation; absent commissioning/rollback/owner acceptance.

npm run release:check is intentionally a NO-GO reporter, not a deployment command
or an automatic approval mechanism. It remains nonzero even if all local checks
eventually pass, because this checkout cannot discharge the external gates.
